input: tca8418: do not leave the chip asserting an edge-triggered line

The TCA8418's INT pin is a level.  The chip holds it asserted for as long
as any status bit is set, and setting a bit that is already set produces no
transition.  The Pyra, like several other boards, wires that pin to a
gpio line described in the device tree as IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_FALLING, and the
driver takes its trigger type from there.

That gives the driver exactly one chance to empty the chip per edge, and
the handler has a window where it can waste it.  tca8418_read_keypad()
drains the FIFO until it reads an empty slot, and only then does the
handler write REG_INT_STAT to acknowledge.  A key pressed between those two
i2c transfers leaves the chip with an event queued and K_INT set again, so
the pin never returns high, so no further edge is ever delivered.  The
keypad is then dead until something clears the chip by other means.  It
logs nothing while this happens, because the handler is never entered.

Seen on a Pyra V5.3: ten keystrokes queued in the FIFO, REG_INT_STAT
reading 0x01, the interrupt not disabled, its count in /proc/interrupts
frozen at 5 for the whole boot, and the keypad completely unresponsive.
Reading the FIFO out and writing REG_INT_STAT by hand over i2c brought it
straight back.

The window is not narrow on this hardware.  The two joystick nubs sit on
the same i2c bus and are polled at 25 ms, so keypad transfers queue behind
them.

Service the chip in a loop instead, and leave only when it reports nothing
pending, so an event that arrives mid-drain is picked up in the same
invocation rather than waiting for an edge that cannot come.  Bound the
loop so that a chip which never goes quiet produces a message rather than
a spinning thread.

Do the same once at the end of probe.  The chip keeps its FIFO and its
status across a driver unbind and across a warm boot, and nothing in the
existing probe path clears them, so a chip that is already asserting when
the driver attaches is dead from that moment.  This was confirmed by
unbinding and rebinding the driver on the stuck chip: REG_INT_STAT still
read 0x01 and the FIFO still held ten events afterwards.

--- a/drivers/input/keyboard/tca8418_keypad.c
+++ b/drivers/input/keyboard/tca8418_keypad.c
@@ -40,6 +40,9 @@
 #define TCA8418_MAX_ROWS	8
 #define TCA8418_MAX_COLS	10
 
+/* How many times to go round before giving up on a chip that will not go quiet */
+#define TCA8418_MAX_SERVICE_PASSES	10
+
 /* TCA8418 register offsets */
 #define REG_CFG			0x01
 #define REG_INT_STAT		0x02
@@ -194,38 +197,74 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * Threaded IRQ handler and this can (and will) sleep.
+ * Service the chip until it stops asserting INT.  Returns true if there was
+ * anything to do.
+ *
+ * INT is a level: the chip holds it asserted for as long as a status bit is
+ * set, and setting a bit again while one is already set produces no edge.  A
+ * board that wires INT to an edge-triggered line therefore gets exactly one
+ * chance to empty the chip.  If a key arrives between the last FIFO read and
+ * the write that clears the status, the chip has a fresh event and an asserted
+ * pin that never returned high, so no further interrupt can ever be delivered
+ * and the keypad is dead until something clears the chip by hand.
+ *
+ * So do not leave until the chip is quiet.
  */
-static irqreturn_t tca8418_irq_handler(int irq, void *dev_id)
+static bool tca8418_service(struct tca8418_keypad *keypad_data)
 {
-	struct tca8418_keypad *keypad_data = dev_id;
+	unsigned int pass = 0;
+	bool handled = false;
 	u8 reg;
 	int error;
 
-	error = tca8418_read_byte(keypad_data, REG_INT_STAT, &reg);
-	if (error) {
-		dev_err(&keypad_data->client->dev,
-			"unable to read REG_INT_STAT\n");
-		return IRQ_NONE;
-	}
+	for (;;) {
+		error = tca8418_read_byte(keypad_data, REG_INT_STAT, &reg);
+		if (error) {
+			dev_err(&keypad_data->client->dev,
+				"unable to read REG_INT_STAT\n");
+			break;
+		}
 
-	if (!reg)
-		return IRQ_NONE;
+		if (!reg)
+			break;
 
-	if (reg & INT_STAT_OVR_FLOW_INT)
-		dev_warn(&keypad_data->client->dev, "overflow occurred\n");
+		handled = true;
 
-	if (reg & INT_STAT_K_INT)
-		tca8418_read_keypad(keypad_data);
+		if (reg & INT_STAT_OVR_FLOW_INT)
+			dev_warn(&keypad_data->client->dev,
+				 "overflow occurred\n");
+
+		if (reg & INT_STAT_K_INT)
+			tca8418_read_keypad(keypad_data);
+
+		/* Clear all interrupts, even IRQs we didn't check (GPI, CAD, LCK) */
+		reg = 0xff;
+		error = tca8418_write_byte(keypad_data, REG_INT_STAT, reg);
+		if (error) {
+			dev_err(&keypad_data->client->dev,
+				"unable to clear REG_INT_STAT\n");
+			break;
+		}
 
-	/* Clear all interrupts, even IRQs we didn't check (GPI, CAD, LCK) */
-	reg = 0xff;
-	error = tca8418_write_byte(keypad_data, REG_INT_STAT, reg);
-	if (error)
-		dev_err(&keypad_data->client->dev,
-			"unable to clear REG_INT_STAT\n");
+		if (++pass >= TCA8418_MAX_SERVICE_PASSES) {
+			dev_err(&keypad_data->client->dev,
+				"chip still asserting after %u passes\n",
+				pass);
+			break;
+		}
+	}
 
-	return IRQ_HANDLED;
+	return handled;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Threaded IRQ handler and this can (and will) sleep.
+ */
+static irqreturn_t tca8418_irq_handler(int irq, void *dev_id)
+{
+	struct tca8418_keypad *keypad_data = dev_id;
+
+	return tca8418_service(keypad_data) ? IRQ_HANDLED : IRQ_NONE;
 }
 
